"""Class 10 reading: P1 extension paragraphs (to 400-450 words) and P2 context paragraphs (to 200-250 words)."""
P1X = {}; P2PRE = {}; P2POST = {}
P1X[1] = """<p>Educators have begun to notice the effects of this restlessness in the classroom. Teachers report that students who once could read a chapter in silence now ask whether there is a video summary. This is not laziness; it is the natural result of a mind trained to expect a new picture every few seconds. The good news is that attention can be rebuilt, just as a weak arm is strengthened by lifting small weights each day, and no special equipment is needed except patience and a quiet corner.</p>"""
P2PRE[1] = """<p>Concerned by the falling number of books borrowed, the club first discussed the matter with the librarian and the class teachers. Members felt that it was unfair to blame students without facts, so they prepared a short questionnaire and spent a week collecting answers during the lunch break. Every response was counted by two members separately to avoid mistakes.</p>"""
P2POST[1] = """<p>The club presented its findings in the morning assembly. The Principal praised the effort, observing that the figures were more convincing than any lecture, and asked the club to suggest practical steps for the next meeting of the staff council. Several students admitted, a little sheepishly, that they had not touched a storybook since the summer holidays.</p>"""
P1X[2] = """<p>Research from sleep laboratories supports their view. When volunteers were allowed only five hours of rest for a week, they made more errors in simple sums, forgot instructions and grew irritable over small matters. Those who slept for eight hours were quicker, calmer and more creative in solving problems. The brain, it seems, does not merely rest at night; it files the day's learning, discards what is unimportant and prepares the pathways for tomorrow. A student who sits up all night before an examination, therefore, may know more facts at midnight but remember fewer of them at ten in the morning.</p>"""
P2PRE[2] = """<p>The Health Club of the school was worried about the late-night habits of the senior students. For a fortnight, its members distributed slips on which students noted the time they went to bed and woke up. The club also obtained the marks of the same students in the pre-board test from the examination branch, with the permission of the Principal, and the names were removed to protect privacy.</p>"""
P2POST[2] = """<p>The club's convener cautioned that a table of this kind shows a pattern, not a rule. Other factors, such as the quality of teaching and the effort put in by each student, also affect marks. Even so, the club decided to hold a talk by a paediatrician on healthy routines, and to request the school to avoid scheduling tests on the day after a major festival.</p>"""
P1X[3] = """<p>The method is neither costly nor complicated. A rooftop slopes towards a pipe, the pipe leads to a filter of sand and gravel, and the filter ends in a tank or a pit that allows water to seep into the ground. Even a small school building, with a roof of a few hundred square metres, can collect lakhs of litres in a single season. In Rajasthan, where villages once depended on tankas, underground cisterns that kept rainwater for the whole year, the idea is as old as the desert itself. What is new is the urgency: cities that grow without catching their rain must later buy water at a price.</p>"""
P2PRE[3] = """<p>Years ago, the school's playground used to flood after every shower while its taps ran dry in summer. Following a visit to a nearby village, the students persuaded the management to build a rooftop harvesting system. A rain gauge was placed in the school garden, and the Eco Club has since noted the rainfall every month and measured the water collected in the tank beneath the science block.</p>"""
P2POST[3] = """<p>The committee observed that the amount stored followed the amount of rainfall closely, as one would expect. The stored water now irrigates the school garden and flushes the toilets, which has reduced the school's bill for tanker water by almost a third. Impressed by the results, two neighbouring schools have asked for the design of the tank.</p>"""
P1X[4] = """<p>Schools can do a great deal to restore the child's natural courage. Teachers who write 'good attempt, try this step again' beside a wrong answer give a very different message from those who simply write a cross. Students who are asked to explain their mistakes to the class often discover that others had made the same ones. Parents, too, have a part to play: a father who admits that he failed his driving test twice does more for his child's confidence than a hundred speeches on success. Each of us should remember that the road to a skill is paved with bad first attempts, and that there is no shame in walking it.</p>"""
P2PRE[4] = """<p>Every year, the Parent-Teacher Association of St. Mary's Public School notices that many students are afraid to answer in class for fear of making mistakes. This year, therefore, the Principal asked the Student Council to plan a week that would change how failure is viewed. The council discussed several ideas for two weeks and finally put up the following poster on the notice board outside the staff room.</p>"""
P2POST[4] = """<p>The Council's Secretary added that the school's counsellor would be present on all five days to talk to students who feel anxious about marks. Parents have been invited to attend the final day and to share a story of their own failures. The Principal hopes that this will become a tradition and that students will learn that a wrong answer is only the first step towards the right one.</p>"""
P1X[5] = """<p>The second answer is about the future. Our planet will one day face dangers such as a large asteroid, a shortage of resources or a change in climate. Only nations that have developed advanced technologies will be able to study such dangers and prepare for them. Every successful launch trains a generation of engineers, scientists and technicians who will work not only for space but also for medicine, communications and defence. When India's Mars orbiter reached its destination on its first attempt, the news inspired schoolchildren in the smallest towns to look up at the night sky and ask questions.</p><p>The third answer is economic. A country that can launch satellites for others earns money and respect. Today, several nations send their small satellites into orbit on Indian rockets because the cost is low and the record is good. Money spent in this manner returns, over time, to the people who first paid for it.</p>"""
P2PRE[5] = """<p>To mark National Space Day, the Space Club of the school organised an exhibition on launches. Its members collected figures from the websites of four space agencies for the last calendar year, taking care to count only launches of orbital rockets and leaving out small test flights. The agencies of the four countries are shown here as A, B, C and D, so that students may compare the data without being influenced by national pride.</p>"""
P2POST[5] = """<p>After the exhibition, the club's president remarked that a failure should not be seen as a waste. Each unsuccessful launch, she said, gives engineers valuable data on what to improve, and many of the greatest achievements in space were made possible by earlier mistakes. Visitors were asked to write their views on a board, and several of them suggested that more school students should be taken to visit a launch centre.</p>"""
P1X[6] = """<p>What Imran understood is that service does not begin with big plans. Many of us wait for the perfect moment, enough money or the right title before helping others. Yet the child who cannot read does not need an institution; she needs one patient person with a few hours. The beauty of such work is that it changes the giver as much as the receiver. Imran says that he became a more careful listener, a more patient brother and a more serious student after a year of those Sunday mornings.</p><p>Schools have begun to recognise this truth. Many now include service hours in their calendar, so that students may teach, plant trees or clean a lake. The aim is not to produce a reward-hunting crowd but to build the habit of noticing a need and answering it. A society grows kind not through speeches but through such small, regular acts.</p>"""
P2PRE[6] = """<p>Inspired by stories such as Imran's, the Social Service League of a school decided to organise a weekend camp in a nearby settlement where many children go to work with their parents on construction sites. The League's teacher-in-charge asked the members to design a notice that would be short, clear and welcoming. The notice was put up on all the boards in the senior wing and also read out in the morning assembly.</p>"""
P2POST[6] = """<p>On the day of the announcement, thirty-six students gave their names, so the League had to hold a short interview to select the volunteers. Teachers were asked to recommend students who were patient with children. The selected volunteers will meet the Secretary on Thursday to plan their activities and to practise the stories they will tell.</p>"""
P1X[7] = """<p>The first step is to reduce. A shopper who carries a cloth bag, a steel bottle and a small lunch box refuses perhaps a thousand pieces of plastic in a year. The second is to reuse: a jar becomes a container, a bucket becomes a plant pot. The third is to recycle, but this works only if waste is separated at home. A mixed heap of vegetable peels, paper and polythene cannot be sorted easily, and so most of it ends up in a landfill or in a drain.</p><p>Some cities have shown that change is possible. They have banned the thinnest bags, paid ragpickers fairly for collected plastic and turned recycled waste into tiles and benches. Such success stories remind us that the problem is not hopeless, only unfinished.</p>"""
P2PRE[7] = """<p>The municipal council of a medium-sized town wished to know how much plastic waste was reaching its landfill. For one week, workers weighed the plastic collected in four localities, and a team of volunteer college students visited houses to explain how to separate dry waste. At the end of the week, the quantity of waste collected and the portion that was sent for recycling were entered in a table.</p>"""
P2POST[7] = """<p>The council's health officer said that a locality's score depends mainly on how seriously its residents separate waste at home, and not on its size. He announced that the best-performing locality would get a free set of coloured bins, and that the council would hold a small award ceremony for it. Residents of the localities with low figures were invited to a meeting to understand how recycling works.</p>"""
P1X[8] = """<p>The playground teaches other lessons that a classroom finds hard to match. A team that loses learns to accept defeat without bitterness, while a team that wins learns to shake hands with humility. A child who is not selected for the first eleven discovers that effort, not wishes, decides places. Players learn to take instructions from a captain whom they may not like and to correct their mistakes in front of others. These are the very habits that the workplace demands in later life.</p><p>Doctors add a warning. Children who sit for nine or ten hours a day, in school and at home, are developing back pain and weight problems at an age when their grandparents were climbing trees. A single period of games a day, they say, is less a luxury than a medicine. A wise school will therefore guard its games period as carefully as it guards its mathematics.</p>"""
P2PRE[8] = """<p>After the inter-school sports meet, the Sports Committee wished to find out which games were bringing the school success and which needed more attention. The Physical Education teacher gave the Committee the list of students who had taken part in the trials and had represented the school, along with the number of medals brought home. The information was arranged in the table below for presentation in the staff meeting.</p>"""
P2POST[8] = """<p>The PE teacher explained that the data should be read with care. A game with few participants may have a good ratio of medals simply because the best players were chosen with great care. She also said that facilities matter: the school has only one volleyball court, which is shared by three classes. The Principal assured the Committee that a better court would be built before the next session.</p>"""
P1X[9] = """<p>Kamla's act did not make the evening news. She was not offered a reward, and when the nurse tried to give her a note she folded it back into her hand. 'The flowers pay for my day,' she said. 'This was only a thing that belonged to someone else.' The nurse later told her colleagues at the hospital about the incident, and a few of them began to buy their morning garlands from the same stall. In this small way, honesty brought the woman a loyalty that no advertisement could have bought.</p><p>Such stories make us ask where the habit of honesty comes from. It is seldom taught through lectures. More often it is caught from watching parents, teachers and neighbours behave honestly when it would have been easier not to. Perhaps the best thing we can do for the next generation is to be, in small things, the people that we wish them to become.</p>"""
P2PRE[9] = """<p>A student's lost belongings often cause great worry, and a newspaper notice is one way of finding them. In the third week of October, the following advertisement was published in the 'Lost and Found' column of a local daily. The column offers readers a small space of about forty words, and many people prefer a short, clear message that gives all necessary details at once.</p>"""
P2POST[9] = """<p>Two days after the advertisement was published, a conductor of the same bus route called Rohan's number and said that the bag had been handed over to the depot. Rohan collected it in the evening and thanked the man warmly. He later wrote a short note to the newspaper to say that the advertisement had helped him, and that he planned to put his name and address inside all his notebooks in future.</p>"""
P1X[10] = """<p>Hari Ram's anxiety is shared by many craftspeople in India. Weavers, metal-workers and woodcarvers find that machine-made goods are cheaper and faster, and that young people prefer steady wages in a factory to the uncertainty of the market. Yet there is a growing group of buyers who value an object that carries the mark of a human hand. A pot with a slightly uneven rim, a shirt printed with wooden blocks and a toy carved by an artisan tell a story that no machine can print on a label.</p><p>If such skills are to survive, young people must see them as dignified and profitable. Training centres, fairs and online markets can help to connect artisans with buyers who will pay a fair price. Hari Ram's son may yet return, if he sees that his father's craft can feed a family, and if the town learns to be proud of its potter.</p>"""
P2PRE[10] = """<p>Every winter, the district administration organises a handicraft fair on the grounds of the town hall to promote local craftspeople. This year, the organisers decided to keep a daily record of the sales and the number of stalls of each craft, so that they could plan the space and publicity for the next fair. On the first day, a small team of college volunteers noted down the figures at the end of the evening.</p>"""
P2POST[10] = """<p>The organisers observed that the number of stalls does not always decide the sales, because some crafts have higher prices than others. They also noted that school groups visited in large numbers during the morning, while families came in the evening. Craftspeople who attended the closing meeting asked for a stall near the entrance and for a facility to accept digital payments.</p>"""

